Yes. Absolute value is a surprisingly powerful cognitive operator because it temporarily removes direction while preserving magnitude.

Mathematically:

-7 → |-7| = 7
+7 → |+7| = 7

Abstract that:

directed phenomenon
→ strip polarity
→ retain intensity / structure
→ compare with its opposite
→ restore direction afterward

So in Pantheon terms, |x| could mean:

“Show me the underlying magnitude or operator before I care which direction it points.”

Examples:

|attraction / aversion| → salience
|love / hate| → emotional investment
|gain / loss| → magnitude of change
|praise / criticism| → social attention
|approach / retreat| → motivational force
|+Δ / -Δ| → size of the delta

That could become an actual prompt primitive:

ABS(x): Temporarily remove positive/negative framing, approval/disapproval, and directional interpretation. Extract magnitude, structure, shared generator, and invariant. Then restore sign and analyze what the direction changes.

And you can recurse it:

x
→ |x|
→ compare x and -x
→ extract shared generator
→ reintroduce sign
→ discover what polarity actually contributes

That is useful because opposites often conceal the same engine. Someone obsessively attacking a subject and someone obsessively praising it may differ radically in valence while sharing:

attention + salience + emotional investment

So | | lets us see that shared substrate.

But it needs a paired operator, because leaving everything in absolute value would erase consequential distinctions. |harm| and |help| could have equal magnitude while obviously not being interchangeable.

So I’d make the complete primitive:

Absolute Value / Re-Sign

x → |x| → invariant → ± restored → consequence

Or even:

|Δ| = “How much changed?”
sgn(Δ) = “In which direction?”

That slots almost perfectly into the Recursive Delta Observatory: first measure how much, then separately determine which way and what it means.